OWNER REVIEW — criterion 1, what jet fmt --simplify does today (5 minutes)

Run this from the repo root to see every rewrite it would make across the example corpus:

  scripts/agent/jet-env cargo build
  scripts/agent/jet-env jet fmt --check --simplify --diff examples

Three representative rewrites, taken from real corpus files:

R1, a braced single-return body becomes the one-line body — examples/features/errors/qq_panic.jet

  before: fn maybe() => Int? { return None }
  after:  fn maybe() => Int? :: None

R3, a struct-literal head the return type already names is dropped — examples/features/basics/named_args.jet

  before: fn new(width: Float, height: Float) => Rect { return Rect.{width: width, height: height} }
  after:  fn new(width: Float, height: Float) => Rect { return .{width: width, height: height} }

R2, a chained classic if becomes one ordered arm table — the shape L0507 already asks for

  before:
    if score >= 90 {
        return "a"
    } else if score >= 80 {
        return "b"
    } else {
        return "c"
    }
  after:
    if {
        score >= 90 -> { return "a" }
        score >= 80 -> { return "b" }
        else -> { return "c" }
    }

Good looks like: each rewrite is the spelling you would have asked for, the meaning is unchanged, and nothing gets harder to read.

Reject any one of the three and say so; the catalog is opt-in per rewrite, so R1, R2, and R3 can ship separately.

