The shared argument parser fills declared positional fields, then ignores every remaining bare value. A wrong invocation exits 0 with default values.

Repro (verified 2026-08-30 on target/debug/jet):

#CLI
struct Args {
    name: String{"world"}
    count: Int{1}
}
fn run(args: Args) { print("{args.name} {args.count}") }

jet run typed.jet -- alice bob -> exit 0, prints "world 1". Both bare values are dropped without any diagnostic. Expected: a parse error in the existing parser voice that names each unconsumed value, plus generated usage, exit 2.

Seam: crates/jet-codegen/src/Prelude/CoreLib/Top/Args.rs:731-762 — after positional filling there is no bare_i < positionals.len() check. The same parser serves #CLI typed entries and the core.args builder, so one fix covers both surfaces. This is a silent wrong-result path on the default tier.

Source: friction audit 2026-08-30, finding Fentry-3.

A file with the documented compiler-discovered build entry reports two problems on plain jet check.

Repro (worker-verified 2026-08-30):

fn build(b: BuildContext) BuildPlan -> {
    return b.plan()
}
fn run() {}

jet check -> L0104 "Private function build is never called" and L0520 "BuildError has no Display impl". Expected: no problems. build is a compiler-discovered root (c0onpxuj), so unused-function analysis must treat it as reachable, and the build-only BuildError should be excluded from the user-facing Display migration lint.

Affects every programmable-build example (fn build appears 20 times repo-wide, examples/features/tooling/programmable_build among them).

Source: friction audit 2026-08-30, finding Fex-core-6.

Build one repository-only semantic CorpusPolicy adapter. This is the closed perimeter that prevents maintained Jet teaching surfaces from reintroducing any of the 38 audited friction findings. It extends existing gates; it is not a user lint engine, a new CLI mode, or a replacement for the lexical retirement ratchet.

Inventory and provenance:
- add one checked-in manifest covering every maintained Jet source role and every producer of maintained generated Jet: executable Jet blocks in docs, site sources, all nested examples and learn solutions, dogfood, gauntlet, agent workloads, performance receipts, package/new-project templates, conformance, UI and fuzz fixture roles, and every binding generator/artifact family;
- classify each entry as canonical teaching, expert lesson, negative diagnostic, raw protocol, fixture/harness boundary, generated output, or non-teaching test data;
- fail when a new eligible file or producer has no manifest row;
- record producer-to-artifact provenance so generated outputs are checked through the generator and cannot drift independently.

One evaluator and registry:
- implement one tests/support CorpusPolicy manifest loader, semantic rule registry, exception validator, and artifact inventory;
- existing golden, dev-corpus, suite-membership, package-template, dogfood/workload, bindgen, and applicable fixture gates invoke that adapter after their own domain checks;
- keep tests/retirement_ratchet.rs lexical and separate;
- consume parsed AST, resolved calls, type/effect/ownership/liveness/constant facts, control-flow facts where named, or generator IR; never regex, line counts, identifier heuristics, or copied walkers.

Rules must cover every original lane finding not made impossible by its product fix. Named rules include: fixed-shape raw CLI and repeated process-boundary policy; typed/builder/diagnostic fixture roles; redundant explicit entry wrappers in ordinary teaching programs; structural Codable; constant-safe duration constructors; redundant inferred effect rows; provably read-only copy sigils; one-child task wrappers; identity error plumbing; indexed sequence loops; covered regex/capture recipes; configured delimited-reader workloads; HTTP wrapper/typed-JSON/unused-request recipes; exact unit/format migration leftovers; duplicate DataTree comparison and explicitly named Tower domain truthiness policies; dogfood JSON, URL query, list equality, directory setup, ASCII/path helpers; and one generated response decoder per actual binding renderer.

Exceptions are occurrence-scoped, not file-scoped: manifest rule + stable semantic site/span + expected occurrence + reason. An additional occurrence fails even when the file already has an exception. Generated protocol overrides also name the protocol identity and prove it differs from the ordinary envelope.

A failure reports file, rule, semantic site/span, why, and mechanical replacement. Arbitrary user projects are unaffected. This card is the recurrence owner for #2375-#2377 and the ratified outcomes on #2378-#2385.
